"803754785360144","KWTA staff to be redeployed immediately, CS Duale assures

Environment, Climate Change and Forestry Cabinet Secretary Hon. Aden Duale has assured that all staff of the defunct Kenya Waters Towers Agency (KWTA) will be redeployed to the Ministry and its agencies based on their identified competencies.

Speaking to staff of KWTA, dissolved in December 2024 after the expiry of its legal mandate, Hon. Duale, who was accompanied by Principal Secretaries Gitonga Mugambi (Forestry) and Dr. Eng. Festus Ng'eno (Environment and Climate Change) said the Ministry had finalized the placement list. 

At the meeting held at the Kenya Meteorological Department (KMD) headquarters in Nairobi, CS Duale announced that 68 and 45 employees will be redeployed to Environment and Climate Change, and Forestry state departments respectively.

In developing the placement list, Hon. Duale said the Ministry considered qualifications, rank, skills, experience and other intervening factors including age and disability if any and current duty station.

At the same time, Hon. Duale rallied the former KWTA staff to strictly observe the public service code of conduct  and principles of good governance as they move into their new stations. 

He urged the staff to strive and maintain a high level of professionalism and integrity.

The defunct KWTA was 
established through Legal Notice No. 27 of 20th April, 2012 with the mandate to 
coordinate and oversee the protection, rehabilitation, conservation and sustainable management of water towers in Kenya.

KWTA was dissolved following the Cabinet sanctioned rationalisation of State Corporations with overlapping and /or duplicate mandates.","English","politics","domestic politics","not foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","mentions sustainability","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","no mention of the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS;ENVIRONMENT"
"790064593746475","Kenya is shifting from a linear waste management model to a circular economy that prioritises waste reduction, reuse, and recycling. This transition is crucial in addressing pollution, creating green jobs, and promoting sustainability.

By implementing waste segregation, composting, and Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) policies, we can transform waste into valuable resources. Innovative startups, green enterprises, and community-led initiatives are driving this change.

Today, Environment and Climate Change Principal Secretary Dr Eng Festus K Ng'eno was represented by Secretary Administration Mr John Elungata in a meeting with the Norwegian delegation led by H.E. Amb. Gunnar Andreas Holm, Norway’s Ambassador to Kenya, during a courtesy visit to the ministry headquarters.

The discussions focused on strengthening collaboration in sustainable waste management, climate change mitigation, and adaptation efforts along Kenya’s coastal region.

This partnership presents an opportunity to enhance Kenya’s waste management efforts by leveraging Norway’s expertise in waste-to-energy solutions, plastic recycling, and deposit-refund systems.

Through financial support, technology transfer, and policy collaboration, Kenya can strengthen its waste management framework, invest in sustainable alternatives, and create more green jobs.

The meeting was attended by NEMA DG Mr Mamo Mamo and Head of the Sustainable Waste Management and Pollution Control Directorate Mr Molu Wato, among others. #CircularEconomy #SustainableWasteManagement
