"1847537896167208","In observance of Pink October, which aims to raise awareness about breast cancer, Her Excellency, the First Lady, presided over the ""Walk for Health"" event organized by Africell and the FABB Foundation in collaboration with One For All. This initiative, titled ""All For One Walk 4 Health The Gambia,"" focused on raising awareness about non-communicable diseases, including breast cancer, and emphasized the importance of physical health.

Her Excellency urged the public to come out in large numbers to participate in the Fight Against Cancer and to get screened to know their health status. She also encouraged everyone to join the Walk for Health, highlighting its benefits for overall well-being. The First Lady expressed her gratitude to all those who participated in making this day a success.","English","politics","domestic politics","not foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","no mention of the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","HEALTH"
"815864554718282","I was delighted to grace the important conference that marks the end of our project on women's political participation and leadership. The goal of women's political participation and leadership is beyond just representation; it is building a just and equitable society where diverse, particularly marginalised voices can contribute to shaping the future.

We celebrate the project's accomplishments and positive impact on women and communities. The project has been a driving force in advancing women's political participation, resulting in a significant increase in women's representation at the local level—an extraordinary achievement for The Gambia. It has empowered more women to run in local government elections, increasing women's representation within community structures.
 
In addition, the project has played a pivotal role in strengthening legal frameworks, policies, gender strategies, and political party bylaws to enhance women's political participation and leadership. This progress is evident in the growing engagement of women, men, youth champions, and advocacy groups pushing for supportive legal reforms.
 
The project has successfully engaged with traditional and religious leaders, men and boys, and women champions, raising awareness about the importance of women's leadership and challenging harmful cultural norms. Furthermore, the project has established a consortium of women who regularly convene to share information and insights through two key initiatives.

While the project officially concludes, its legacy will live on through the women it has empowered, the structures it has built, and the positive changes it has catalysed. The journey towards gender equality in political leadership is ongoing, and the WPL project has laid a strong foundation for continued progress. I wish to appeal to the UN agencies and the peacebuilding fund to ensure we maintain this momentum as we journey toward 2026, that this effort is sustained, and that more women are prepared to come out and be elected to political office.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","positive towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","positive towards democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","CIVIL RIGHTS;GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS"
