The National Minister of Health, 𝗛𝗼𝗻. 𝗬𝗼𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗮 𝗔𝘄𝗲𝗹 𝗗𝗲𝗻𝗴, has addressed concerns regarding the flow of humanitarian aid to South Sudan. In a recent statement following her return from the UN General Assembly, 𝗛𝗼𝗻. 𝗬𝗼𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗱𝗮 𝗔𝘄𝗲𝗹 𝗗𝗲𝗻𝗴 clarified that international donors do not remit funds directly to government institutions. Instead, 𝙩𝙝𝙚𝙨𝙚 𝙛𝙪𝙣𝙙𝙨 𝙖𝙧𝙚 𝙘𝙝𝙖𝙣𝙣𝙚𝙡𝙚𝙙 𝙩𝙝𝙧𝙤𝙪𝙜𝙝 𝙞𝙢𝙥𝙡𝙚𝙢𝙚𝙣𝙩𝙞𝙣𝙜 𝙥𝙖𝙧𝙩𝙣𝙚𝙧𝙨 𝙩𝙤 𝙚𝙣𝙨𝙪𝙧𝙚 𝙚𝙛𝙛𝙚𝙘𝙩𝙞𝙫𝙚 𝙖𝙣𝙙 𝙚𝙛𝙛𝙞𝙘𝙞𝙚𝙣𝙩 𝙙𝙚𝙡𝙞𝙫𝙚𝙧𝙮 𝙤𝙛 𝙝𝙪𝙢𝙖𝙣𝙞𝙩𝙖𝙧𝙞𝙖𝙣 𝙖𝙨𝙨𝙞𝙨𝙩𝙖𝙣𝙘𝙚.

The Minister explained that South Sudan operates under a zero-cash policy, which restricts direct government dealings with donors. This policy is in place to mitigate financial risks and maintain transparency in the aid distribution process. She emphasized that the decision to implement this policy was made in 2013, following the imposition of financial sanctions on the country.

In response to questions about the apparent discrepancy between the substantial influx of humanitarian aid and the ongoing humanitarian crisis, Minister Awel reiterated that the funds are being utilized to support critical services and programs. She highlighted the efforts of implementing partners in providing essential healthcare, food security, and other vital assistance to vulnerable populations.

The Minister's statement serves to shed light on the complex funding mechanisms in place for humanitarian aid in South Sudan. By clarifying the role of implementing partners and the reasons for the 𝕫𝕖𝕣𝕠-𝕔𝕒𝕤𝕙 𝕡𝕠𝕝𝕚𝕔𝕪, she aims to increase public understanding and trust in the aid delivery process.

