"689116706929309","The Validation of the strategic plan 2025 – 2029 for Brikama Area Council.
As the validation for the strategic plan continues, on Thursday 21st November 2024, the Directorate of Development Planning under the Ministry of Finance and Economic Affairs and United Nations Development Program (UNDP) ended a two-day validation for the strategic plan 2025-2029 for Brikama Area Council.
The event brought together key stakeholders, including councilors and senior management of the municipality, representatives of Women, Children, Youth, Persons with Disabilities, Alkalo’s and others to collaboratively review, and validate the strategic roadmap. 
This session will continue in Kuntaur and Basse Area Councils respectively next week.
Day1 of the session, which was on Wednesday 20th November, Focused on:
• Presentation and review of the draft strategic plan.
• Fixing the Results matrix of the council for M&E and measuring progress
• Councilors and stakeholders feedback session.
• Alkalo’s feedback session.
• West Africa Coastal Area has an initiative to build resilience to communities around the coastal areas in Brikama Area.
• Ways to tackle deforestation and flooding in BAC.

Day2 of the session, which was on Thursday 21st November 2024, focuses on:
• Feedback from Day 1 into the revised version of the strategic plan.
• Breakout sessions to delve deeper into key thematic areas under the BAC such as Sustainability, Health care facilities and trained SRN’s, Flood mitigation, Deforestation and Borehole building, Workforce development, economic growth, organizational effectiveness and resource mobilization, Crime rates, youth employment etc.
• The Financial plan which will finance the implementation of the strategic plan was also filled by various sectors in BAC.

The two-day validation workshop was a resounding success, fostering collaboration and ensuring a shared commitment to the strategic plan for BAC 2025-2029 and alignment to the RF-NDP 2023-2027. The Strategic plan will provide a clear and actionable roadmap for achieving the council’s plan in the next five years. 
The Strategic Plan will serve as the basis for the council’s journey towards a sustainable future.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","resource distribution","national government;international organisations","specific locality or group","not applicable","Brikama Area Council","resources for women","mentions climate change","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","positive towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","subgroup unity","Brikama Area","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS;ENVIRONMENT"
"3386962018128129","The Validation of the strategic plan 2025 – 2029 for Basse Area Council.
On Thursday 28th November 2024, the Directorate of Development Planning under the Ministry of Finance and Economic Affairs and United Nations Development Program (UNDP) ended the validation for the strategic plan 2025-2029 in Basse Area Council.
The event brought together key stakeholders, including the Vice chairman or BSAC, the CEO, councilors, representatives of Women, Children and Youths, representative of the Arm forces, Persons with Disabilities and other Members to collaboratively review, and validate the strategic roadmap. 
Day1 of the session, which was on Wednesday 27th November, Focused on:
• Presentation and review of the draft strategic plan.
• Assigning members into various groups to complete the Results matrix of the council for M&E and measuring progress of the key areas of focus for Basse Area Council.
• Councilors and stakeholders feedback session.
• Officers comment on traffic incidents and ways to minimize it.

Day2 of the session, which was on Thursday 28th November 2024, focuses on:
• Feedback from Day 1 into the revised version of the strategic plan.
• Breakout sessions to delve deeper into five (5) key thematic areas under the Basse Area Council such as Health and Education (Human Capital), Services (access to clean water, road constructions rehabilitation and maintenance, traffic flow), Climate change and environment, Economic growth and Effective resource mobilization, Finance.
• The Financial plan which will finance the implementation of the strategic plan was also filled by various sectors in Basse Area Council. 

The two-day validation workshop was a resounding success, fostering collaboration and ensuring a shared commitment to the strategic plan for Basse Area Council 2025-2029 and alignment to the RF-NDP 2023-2027. The Strategic plan will provide a clear and actionable roadmap for achieving the council’s plan in the next five years. 
The Strategic Plan will serve as the basis for the council’s journey towards a sustainable future.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","resource distribution","national government;international organisations","specific locality or group","not applicable","Basse Area Cuncil","resources for women","mentions climate change","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","positive towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","subgroup unity","Basse Area","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","GOVERNMENT OPERATIONS;ENVIRONMENT;EDUCATION"
"1313380823526881","Allocation of US$ 27.1M to Basic, Sec. Education Sector Shows Gov’t’s Commitment to Human Capital Dev’t – Minister Keita 

The  Minister of Finance and Economic Affairs (MoFEA) Seedy K.M. Keita, has affirmed The Gambia Government's commitment to investing in the country's human capital, highlighting the importance of access to appropriate, current, and high-quality learning materials, especially textbooks, for a responsible education system.

The Minister made this remarks during a recent press conference his ministry jointly organised with the Ministry of Basic and Secondary Education (MoBSE) and the World Bank.

The Basic and Secondary Education Ministry is set to hand over of 1.47 million textbooks to be distributed across the country at no cost to students.

Dr. Habibatou Drammeh, Minister of Basic Secondary Education, expressed joy in receiving these important learning materials for basic and secondary education.
 
She stressed that quality education is essential for nation building and equipping children with essential tools for success. The RISE project aims to improve foundational learning for early childhood development to grade 9, ensuring that children and youth are equipped to succeed.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","resource distribution","national government;international organisations","country-wide","not applicable","not applicable","resources not specifically for women","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","positive towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","EDUCATION;ECONOMY"
"1432153798079932","The Minister of Finance and Economic Affairs, Hon. Seedy K.M. Keita, who also serves as the Chairman of the Board of Governors of the ECOWAS Bank for Investment and Development (EBID), hosted an EBID field mission to The Gambia from 26th  January to 6th February 2025. The meeting took place on Monday, 3rd February 2025.
As part of the rotation principle established at the first Board of Governors meeting in Lomé, Togo, on 22nd August 2003, The Gambia is set to host the upcoming 23rd Annual General Meeting (AGM) of the Board of Governors, scheduled for 14th April 2025 in Banjul.

The objective of the field mission was to engage with Minister Keita on key preparations for the AGM. The Minister welcomed the delegation led by Mr. Abdoulaye Coulibaly, Secretary General of EBID, who attended as a special envoy of the Bank’s President, Dr. George Donkor.

During the meeting, Mr. Coulibaly outlined the key logistical arrangements for the AGM, including local transportation, accommodation, and necessary equipment. Accompanying him was Mr. Sylvester Gusso, Director at EBID, who attended discussions at the Ministry of Finance and Economic Affairs.

The engagement underscores The Gambia’s commitment to ensuring a successful and well-organized AGM, reinforcing its role in regional economic cooperation and development.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","no mention of the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","african unity","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS;ECONOMY"
"1643957276963496","Ministry of Health Commemorates Breastfeeding Week

... Launches New Nursing Room
(September 6, 2024, Bopolu, Gbarpolu County)The Ministry of Health (MOH) in collaboration with World Health Organization (WHO), UNICEF, World Bank and other partners on Friday, September 6, 2024, jointly observed the World Breastfeeding Week at the Bapolu City Hall in Bopolu City, Gbarpolu County. 

World Breastfeeding Week is a global campaign held in the first week of August each year to break inequalities in breastfeeding globally and raise awareness on the importance of baby mothers’ breastfeeding. 

The day celebrates breastfeeding mothers in all their diversity, throughout their breastfeeding journeys, and showcases the ways families, societies, communities and health workers can have the back of every breastfeeding mothers. 

This year breastfeeding week observance was held under the Theme: “Closing The Gap: Breastfeeding Support For All.”","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","resource distribution","national government;international organisations","specific locality or group","not applicable","Bopolu; Gbarpolu Country","resources for women","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","positive towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","NOT CODED","HEALTH"
"800558132325247","World Bank Finance Project Donates Medical Supplies to MOH

(Monrovia, October 15, 2024 )– The Ministry of Health (MOH) has received assorted medical supplies from the World Bank Finance Project, including 300 cartons of medical supplies, 150 mattresses, and 150 Intensive Care Unit (ICU) beds with accessories.

These essential medical items will be distributed to various Intensive Care Units (ICUs) in rural communities, where they will be used to support critical healthcare services.

The Ministry of Health remains committed to improving the health and well-being of all Liberian citizens and residents. Through comprehensive policies and strategic initiatives, the Ministry continues to strive for accessible, affordable, and high-quality healthcare services throughout the country.
