"1287494473274812","Minister of Industrialisation, Business, Trade and Tourism George Patridge has called for a reset of Malawi’s trade strategy to curb the widening trade deficit, as the Export Development Fund (EDF) launched the inaugural Malawi Trade Report 2025 in Lilongwe on Tuesday.

The report was unveiled at the Bingu International Conference Centre (BICC) during an event attended by government officials, development partners, private sector representatives and the academia.

“There are other things that are on the report but they are not fully implemented on the ground and we will make sure that they are implemented so that we can narrow the trade deficit.” 

He further called on authorities to reset Malawi’s trade strategy to avoid a continued trade deficit, noting that exports fell by 7.1 percent in 2024, while imports hit about US$3.3 billion, creating a trade deficit of roughly US$2.4 billion.

 ""Our task is clear: we must achieve structural transformation by expanding production capacity, deepening value addition, and leveraging new markets, particularly regional markets where Malawi holds clear advantages,""  he said.

EDF Board Chairperson Ted Nankhumwa said the Malawi Trade Report 2025 is designed to provide evidence-based analysis to guide decisions by both government and the private sector.

Representing the private sector, Willard Mpinganjira from the financial services hailed the report, saying it clearly shows where businesses can intervene.

“The publication has given us room to see where we can come in, the opportunities that are there and what we can practically do,” said Mpinganjira.

The report shows that Malawi remains heavily dependent on primary agricultural exports and continues to underperform in global trade, but also points to emerging opportunities in services, including ICT and transport.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","no mention of the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","no mention of the nation or national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","no mention of africa","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","not specifically patriotic","DOMESTIC COMMERCE;ECONOMY;AGRICULTURE"
