"904705952337259","𝐂𝐇𝐈𝐋𝐃𝐑𝐄𝐍 𝐂𝐑𝐈𝐄𝐒 𝐓𝐎 𝐁𝐄 𝐇𝐄𝐀𝐑𝐃

Gaborone, Botswana: Botswana's Child President, Her Excellency Ms. Aumakwe Musonda Aaron, has stressed the urgent need for governments to invest meaningfully in children. Speaking during the commemoration of the Sub-Regional World Children's Day held at the Parliament chambers today under the theme, ""My Day, My Rights, from Promises to Actions"", Ms. Aaron highlighted children must be heard and included in decisions that affect their lives.

Ms. Aaron called on governments to prioritise education and health and create a safe environment for children. She stated that in many African countries, children continue to face significant constraints that hinder their access to quality education, citing acute shortage of educators, under-equipped libraries, dilapidated learning infrastructure and exclusion of learners with disabilities as some issues affecting the region.

Ms. Aaron also said rising cases of social ills such as teenage pregnancy, early child marriages and defilement poses mental and psychological threat to the well-being of children ,  she challenged governments to devise stiffer punishment for perpetrators and to expand child protection services .

Ms. Aaron appealed for collective action from all stakeholders in addressing the needs of children, highlighting that  children's safety and protection are a shared responsibility and not the duty of leaders only. 

In his address, President Advocate Duma Gideon Boko said this year's theme is a call to governments, communities and global leaders to move beyond promises and deliver tangible change for vulnerable children facing barriers in education, health, safety and freedom. ""We are here to reaffirm that the rights of every child matter, that every child deserves to grow up safe, loved and free to dream and that the voices of our children matter,"" he said.

The children's parliamentary session ended with President Advocate Boko handing over the baton to Lesotho, the next host nation. The event was graced by representatives from nine countries being Botswana, Zimbabwe, Zambia, Mozambique, Lesotho, South Africa, Namibia, Malawi and Kingdom of Eswatini.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","not resource distribution","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","not applicable","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","no mention of the China","neutral towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","positive towards democracy","not anti-western","national unity","subgroup unity","learners wit disabilities excluded from quality education in African countries","african unity","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","not specifically patriotic","CIVIL RIGHTS;EDUCATION;SOCIAL WELFARE"
"2.13847E+15","O Presidente da República, José Maria Neves, reforçou, em entrevista ao jornalista Carlos Santos, para a RDP-África, o seu sentimento de pesar pela morte de Odair Mendonça Moniz, cidadão cabo-verdiano vítima de disparos de um agente da Polícia de Segurança Pública portuguesa, no bairro da Cova da Moura, na cidade de Amadora. 

O Chefe de Estado lamenta ainda  os acontecimentos em resultad dessa situação e deixa o seu veemente repúdio pelos discursos anti-emigrantes em torno deste caso  em Portugal. 

José Maria Neves pede bom senso e equilíbrio no tratamento da questão. 

O Mais Alto Magistrado da Nação revelou que já falou sobre a morte de Odair Moniz com o embaixador de Cabo Verde em Lisboa, Eurico Monteiro, e manifestou solidariedade aos familiares da vítima. 

A embaixada está a acompanhar de perto a situação, garantindo apoio à família da vítima e à comunidade, apelando à serenidade e à calma.

""Hoje ainda mandei uma mensagem à Associação da Cova da Moura e aos familiares da vítima, manifestando a minha solidariedade, apresentando os meus pêsames e apelando sempre à serenidade e à calma"", concluiu o Presidente.

