"1217986519754071","The Chief's Diary: Ongoing Implementation of Recommendations of the Tripartite Report: #WeAreDelivering  

Today, I hosted the quarterly Steering Committee meeting for the Implementation of the Tripartite Report. It was well attended by several members from APPA, APWA, Mayor of Bo, National Council of Paramount Chiefs, Sierra Leone Bar Association, and two CSOs (CHRDI and Women’s Forum). Though we had a quorum, we missed the Mayor of Freetown, Bonthe, Bombali and Kenema, and the leaders of the majority and minority parties in Parliament (the latter sent a representative). 

In these meetings, we discuss updates, review roadmaps, and delegate new responsibilities. 

A major update today is that GoSL has continued to support the Secretariat by seconding staff and providing start-up operational capital. Some of our development partners are also providing technical assistance to institutions of governance to support their own implementation responsibilities.

More specifically and significantly, I am happy to report that we now have a preliminary synthesis report on the Independent Management and Functional Review of ECSL completed. 

Why is this important?

Every major political actor, leader, and party has called for the independent review of ECSL since this institution was established (INEC in 1995, NEC in 2002). This is the first time, since the establishment of that institution that this process of a whole scale ""independent review"" has happened, thanks to the leadership of President Bio. We would like to thank our development partners for standing by with us and providing technical support through USAID, ECOWAS, UN, EU, etc. The nearest we've ever come to this transformational activity was prior to the 2007 elections when IFES supported a restructuring of NEC. We can't wait to see the report.

As Chair, I have been advised by the PSRU team that the Final Management and Functional Review Report of the ECSL will be submitted to me by the 25th of March, 2025. This is well in line with the commitments I had given to the president, cabinet, and the people of Sierra Leone. 

Of further importance is that the PSRU has received  support from the GoSL for the review of other independent election management bodies, including the PPRC, the Independent Police Complaints Board (IPCB), the Independent Media Commission (IMC) and the Sierra Leone Broadcasting Corporation (SLBC).

