"2.45861E+15","President Dr Nangolo Mbumba holds bilateral talks with President  #XiJinping of the People’s Republic of #China and concludes participation in the 2024 Summit of the 9th Forum on China-Africa Cooperation, Beijing, China
 
The President of the Republic of Namibia, H.E Dr Nangolo Mbumba concluded on the 6th  of September 2024, a four day working visit to the People’s Republic of China, which included participation in the 2024 Summit of the 9th Forum on China-Africa Cooperation and a bilateral meeting with President Xi Jinping of China. On 3 September 2024, President Mbumba held meetings with several Chinese multinational entreprises with an interest to invest in various sectors of the Namibian economy, including infrastructure, manufacturing, mining, tourism, digital technologies and renewable energy. President Nangolo Mbumba also undertook a visit to the global technology company Huawei where the President was hosted by the Chairman of the Huawei Board, Mr. Liang Hua. President Mbumba was taken through various technological and digital products of the company, including in the fields of smart cities, digital governance and communication technologies. Huawei also shared a presentation focussed on training and capacity building of young Namibians and schools.
 
On 4 September 2024, President Mbumba delivered keynote remarks at the Namibia-China High-Level Business Forum, which attracted diverse business leaders with existing and potential investments in Namibia. Throughout the engagments with Chinese business leaders and investors, President Mbumba encouraged Chinese companies to focus on win-win partnerships and in pursuance of mutually beneficial investments, which contribute to Namibia’s developmental agenda. The President commended those companies that are already making tangible investments that are leading to the amelioration of the living conditions of Namibians through  economic growth and assisting the country to fight youth unemployment. During the Business Forum, emphasizing the resolve of Namibia to economic self-reliance, President Mbumba highlighted key sectors of priority, which include mineral beneficiation and value-addition, renewable energy, chemicals, cosmetics and agro-processing. President Mbumba also informed Chinese business leaders that the Government was calibrating sectoral competitiveness and will launch the Namibia Trade Policy, which forms the cornerstone of our strategy for economic growth and industrial development. “The goal is to establish new industries that will serve as magnets for fresh investments and skilled personnel, enhancing our nation's appeal”, President Mbumba said. 
 
During the FOCAC Summit on 5 September 2024, President Nangolo Mbumba, consistent with Namibia’s priority to enhance food security, participated and delivered a statement during the FOCAC 2024 “High-Level Meeting on Industrialization and Agricultural Modernisation” convening under the theme “Industrialisation, Agricultural Modernisation and Green Development: Routes to Modernisation”. Highlighting the need to learn from China’s rapid industrialisation and agricultural modernisation, President Mbubma informed the meeting, “there can be no sustainable industrisalisation without addressing the reforms of the international financial architecture. Mobilising international finance should be made easy and accessible in order to assist developing countries achieve their National Development Plans and ensure the collective realization of the Sustainable Development Goals by 2030.”  President Nangolo Mbumba also informed the meeting that Namibia would co-host alongside Germany the Summit of the Future during the 79th High-Level Segment of the United Nations General Assembly, which provides an opportunity for the United Nations to reach consensus on how best to deal with the challenges of industrialization and development.
 
The President further highlighted the initiatives Namibia was undertaking the plans to establish agro-processing centres, particularly in the rural areas, for youth, cooperatives and farmers in general. Underlining the need to source technologies from China, the objective was to add value to agricultural produce; to develop new products, contribute to economic activities in the remote areas, and to stimulate economic growth, the President said.
 
In the afternoon of 5 September, President Nangolo Mbumba held bilateral talks with President Xi Jinping at the Great Hall of the People. Applauding President Xi for the successful 2024 FOCAC Summit, President said that the Comprehensive Strategic Partnership for Cooperation provided Namibia and China with a framework for the developmental aspirations of both countries to be met, specifically for Namibia to learn from China in the areas of Technical and Vocational Education and Training of Namibian Youth; Information, Communication Technology, and foreign direct investment through leading Chinese businesses.
 
President Mbumba informed his Chinese counterpart, President Xi that Namibia wanted to work closely with China to develop green hydrogen, solar manufacturing, hydro, wind, and nuclear energy. President Mbumba said that Namibia wanted to scale up infrastructures, including a desalination plant and nuclear infrastructure to add value to uranium for the peaceful development of nuclear energy and other energy sources. Moreover, President Mbumba said that the Namibian Government was seeking cooperation with China to ensure national safety through the implementation of technology based Smart Cities.
 
During the bilateral talks, President Xi committed to assist the Namibian Government through a grant of approximately 1 Billion Namibian Dollars to aid the effors of the Namibian Government to provide housing to police officers and informed that a technical team would be send to Namibia to undertake feasibility studies for the project. During the talks, President Xi also availed close to 500 Million Namibian Dollars in grants for future projects. Additionally, the Government  of China committed close to 50 Million Namibian Dollars worth of assistance to augment the efforts of the Namibian Government toward drought relief. Expressing condolonces following the passing of President Hage G. Geingob, who he said was his friend, President Xi applauded President Mbumba for the steady leadership he had provided to the Namibian people during the difficult period of mourning and beyond.
 
President Mbumba concluded FOCAC 2024 engagements and the working visit to China with a meeting and a banquet in his honor with His Excellency Cai Qi, Member of the Standing Committee of the Political Bureau of the CPC Central Committee and Member of the Secretariat of the CPC Central Committee.
 
President Nangolo Mbumba returned to Namibia on 6 September 2024.","English","politics","domestic politics","foreign politics","resource distribution","other state","country-wide;specific locality or group","not applicable","not applicable","resources not specifically for women","mentions sustainability","no mention of the US","no mention of the Russia","positive towards China","neutral towards the UN","no mention of the IMF","no mention of democracy","not anti-western","national unity","no mention of specific subgroup","not applicable","african unity","no mention of political opponents","no mention of religion","no mention of violent group","not specifically patriotic","INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS;ECONOMY;ENERGY"
"901250645849947","𝐄́𝐯𝐚𝐥𝐮𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧 𝐟𝐢𝐧𝐚𝐥𝐞 𝐝𝐞𝐬 𝐂𝐨𝐧𝐭𝐫𝐚𝐭s 𝐀𝐧𝐧𝐮𝐞𝐥𝐬 𝐝𝐞 𝐏𝐞𝐫𝐟𝐨𝐫𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞 (𝐂𝐀𝐏) :𝐌𝐢𝐧𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐞̀𝐫𝐞 𝐝𝐞𝐬 𝐌𝐢𝐧𝐞𝐬 𝐞𝐭 𝐝𝐞 𝐥𝐚 𝐆𝐞́𝐨𝐥𝐨𝐠𝐢𝐞

Conakry, 21 novembre 2025 – Le Ministère des Mines et de la Géologie a présenté ce vendredi son évaluation finale devant le Comité chargé du suivi des CAP. Représentant le département, le Secrétaire général Aboubacar Kourouma a annoncé un taux de réalisation de 87 %, malgré des contraintes budgétaires et opérationnelles.

Il a mis en avant plusieurs avancées majeures, notamment la concrétisation du projet Simandou, la progression de Nimba Mining, ainsi que l’intensification des recherches géologiques. Le Ministère souligne également les efforts engagés pour le suivi des activités minières, l’assainissement du cadastre minier – avec plus de 400 permis retirés – et la régulation de l’orpaillage.

